tasks.prompt:
Write a Python function flatten_keys(d: dict) -> dict that flattens a nested structure of dicts and lists into a single-level dict. Nested dict keys are joined with '.', and list positions are written as '[i]' appended to the key, so {'a': {'b': 1, 'c': [2, {'d': 3}]}} becomes {'a.b': 1, 'a.c[0]': 2, 'a.c[1].d': 3}. All keys are strings. An empty dict or empty list encountered as a value is kept as that value under its key. The top-level input is always a dict.

Visible examples (your function must pass these):
assert flatten_keys({'a': {'b': 1, 'c': [2, {'d': 3}]}}) == {'a.b': 1, 'a.c[0]': 2, 'a.c[1].d': 3}
assert flatten_keys({}) == {}

Reply with the complete function definition in one ```python code block (imports included). Brief prose around it is fine. The block must pass the visible examples and will be run against additional hidden tests.
